[2][3] You need to create and register your subscriber class as well as configure the dependency injection container to tag your subscriber with `kernel.event_subscriber`. First, ensure you have a subscriber class implementing `EventSubscriberInterface` like this example:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Subscriber/MySubscriber.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);
namespace Swag\BasicExample\Subscriber;
use Shopware\Core\Content\Product\ProductEvents;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\Event\EntityLoadedEvent;
use Symfony\Component\EventDispatcher\EventSubscriberInterface;

class MySubscriber implements EventSubscriberInterface {
    public static function getSubscribedEvents(): array {
        return [
            ProductEvents::PRODUCT_LOADED_EVENT => 'onProductsLoaded',
        ];
    }
}
```
Then, register your subscriber in the `services.php` file of your plugin:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Resources/config/services.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);
use Swag\BasicExample\Subscriber\MySubscriber;
use Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\Loader\Configurator\ContainerConfigurator;

return static function (ContainerConfigurator $configurator): void {
    $services = $configurator->services();
    $services->set(MySubscriber::class)
        ->tag('kernel.event_subscriber');
};
```
Make sure your subscriber class is correctly implemented and registered. If you still have issues, check for any errors or warnings in the Shopware logs.

To read a value from your plugin's configuration inside a PHP service, you can use the `SystemConfigService` provided by Shopware. Here is how you can inject and use it in your subscriber:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Resources/config/services.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);
use Shopware\Core\System\SystemConfig\SystemConfigService;
use Swag\BasicExample\Subscriber\MySubscriber;
use Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\Loader\Configurator\ContainerConfigurator;

return static function (ContainerConfigurator $configurator): void {
    $services = $configurator->services();

    $services->set(MySubscriber::class)
        ->args([service(SystemConfigService::class)]);
};
``` 

Then, in your subscriber class:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Subscriber/MySubscriber.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);
namespace Swag\BasicExample\Subscriber;
use Shopware\Core\System\SystemConfig\SystemConfigService;

class MySubscriber implements EventSubscriberInterface {
    private SystemConfigService $systemConfigService;

    public function __construct(SystemConfigService $systemConfigService)
    {
        $this->systemConfigService = $systemConfigService;
    }

    public static function getSubscribedEvents(): array
    {
...
    }
}
```

You can then use the `get` method to read configuration values. For example, to read a value with the technical name 'example', you would do:

```php
public function onProductsLoaded(EntityLoadedEvent $event): void {
    $exampleConfig = $this->systemConfigService->get('SwagBasicExample.config.example', $salesChannelId);
}
```

Remember to set `salesChannelId` to `null` to apply the configuration to all Sales Channels, or pass a specific Sales Channel ID.

To create a scheduled task in a plugin, you need to define two main classes along with their respective service tags:

1. **ScheduledTask** class (e.g., `ExampleTask`):
   - Extends from `Shopware\Core\Framework\MessageQueue\ScheduledTask\ScheduledTask`
   - Implements the required methods: `getTaskName()` and `getDefaultInterval()`
2. ScheduledTaskHandler class (e.g., `ExampleTaskHandler`):
   - Extends from `Shopware\Core\Framework\MessageQueue\ScheduledTask\ScheduledTaskHandler`
   - Optionally implements `Shopware\Core\Framework\MessageQueue\ScheduledTask\DynamicallyScheduledTaskHandler` interface for dynamic rescheduling

You also need to register these classes in your plugin's `services.php` file with the following service tags:
- `shopware.scheduled.task`: Registers the task definition.
- `messenger.message_handler`: Registers the handler as a Messenger consumer for the task.

Here is an example of how you might set this up in your `src/Resources/config/services.php` file:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Resources/config/services.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);
use Swag\BasicExample\Service\ScheduledTask\ExampleTask;
use Swag\BasicExample\Service\ScheduledTask\ExampleTaskHandler;
use Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\Loader\Configurator\ContainerConfigurator;

return static function (ContainerConfigurator $configurator): void {
    $services = $configurator->services();

    $services->set(ExampleTask::class)
        ->tag('shopware.scheduled.task');

    $services->set(ExampleTaskHandler::class)
        ->args([
            service('scheduled_task.repository'),
            service('logger'),
        ])
        ->tag('messenger.message_handler');
};
```

[3] To add your own command to `bin/console` from a plugin, you need to register it as a service in the plugin's `src/Resources/config/services.php`. Here is an example of how to do this for a custom command named `ExampleCommand`:

```php
// src/Command/ExampleCommand.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);
namespace Swag\basicExample\Command;

use Symfony\Component\Console\Command\Command;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Input\InputInterface;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Output\OutputInterface;

class ExampleCommand extends Command {
    protected function execute(InputInterface $input, OutputInterface $output): int {
        $output->writeln('Hello from ExampleCommand');
        return Command::SUCCESS;
    }
}
``` 

Then you can tag the service with `console.command` to make it available via `bin/console`. Here is how your services configuration might look:

```php
// src/Resources/config/services.php
$services->set(Swag\basicExample\Command\ExampleCommand::class)
    ->tag('console.command');
```

Now, the command can be run using `bin/console swag:example` where `swag` is the name of your plugin and `example` is the name you gave to your command. [2] You also need to ensure that your scheduled task or custom commands are properly registered so they can be executed as needed.

To pass extra data from your plugin to a storefront pagelet like the footer and display it in Twig, you need to follow these steps:

1. **Subscribe to an Event**: You need to subscribe to the `FooterPageletLoadedEvent` event to add custom data to the footer.
2. **Register Your Subscriber**: Register your subscriber in the DI-Container with the tag 'kernel.event_subscriber'.
3. **Add Data to Footer**: Override the footer template and render the additional data using a variable that points to the footer object, which contains the path `extensions.product_count.count` for displaying the count of active products.

Here's how you can implement these steps:

### Step 1: Subscribe to an Event
```php [PLUGIN_ROOT/src/Service/AddDataToPage.php]
namespace Swag\BasicExample\Service;
use Shopware\Storefront\Pagelet\Footer\FooterPageletLoadedEvent;
use Symfony\Component\EventDispatcher\EventSubscriberInterface;

class AddDataToPage implements EventSubscriberInterface {
    public static function getSubscribedEvents(): array
    {
        return [
            FooterPageletLoadedEvent::class => 'addActiveProductCount',
        ];
    }

    public function addActiveProductCount(FooterPageletLoadedEvent $event): void
    {
        // Add your logic to set the data here.
    }
}
```

### Step 2: Register Your Subscriber
```php [PLUGIN_ROOT/Resources/config/services.php]
$services->set(\
    \Swag\BasicExample\Service\AddDataToPage::class\
)->tag('kernel.event_subscriber');
```

### Step 3: Display the Data in Twig
Override the footer template and add a new column to display the count of active products.
```twig [PLUGIN_ROOT/Resources/views/storefront/layout/footer/footer.html.twig]
{% sw_extends '@Storefront/storefront/layout/footer/footer.html.twig' %}

{% block layout_footer_navigation_columns %}
    {{ parent() }}

    {% if footer.extensions.product_count %}
        <div class="col-md-4 footer-column">
            <p>This shop offers you {{ footer.extensions.product_count.count }} products</p>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}
```

By following these steps, your plugin will be able to add custom data (like the count of active products) to the footer pagelet and display it in the Storefront.

To define a custom entity for your own database table with the Data Abstraction Layer (DAL), you need to create an `EntityDefinition` class. This class extends from `Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityDefinition`, which requires implementing two methods: `getEntityName` and `defineFields`. The method `getEntityName` returns a string representing your table name, such as 'swag_example'. The method `defineFields` contains the fields that your entity or table consists of. Here's an example of how to create an `ExampleDefinition.php` file in your plugin directory for defining a custom entity named 'swag_example':

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Core/Content/Example/ExampleDefinition.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1)=namespace Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityDefinition;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\FieldCollection;

class ExampleDefinition extends EntityDefinition
{
    public const ENTITY_NAME = 'swag_example';

    public function getEntityName(): string
    {
        return self::ENTITY_NAME;
    }

    protected function defineFields(): FieldCollection
    {
        return new FieldCollection([]);
    }
}
```

To fetch products by their name using the product repository, you can create a criteria object and add a filter to search for products with a specific name. Here's how you can do it:

```php
public function readData(Context $context): void {
    $criteria = new Criteria();
    $criteria->addFilter(new EqualsFilter('name', 'Example name'));

    $products = $this->productRepository->search($criteria, $context);
}
```

This example will search for all products with the name `Example name` and return an `EntitySearchResult` containing all matched products. Since the `EntitySearchResult` is extending the `EntityCollection`, which is iterable, you could iterate over the results using a `foreach`. You can find more examples of available filters in our [DAL reference about filters](../../../../development/troubleshooting/dal-reference/filters-reference.md).

You can also combine multiple filters to achieve more complex queries. For example, if you want to fetch products that either have an ID or the mentioned name:

```php
public function readData(Context $context): void {
    $criteria = new Criteria();
    $criteria->addFilter(new OrFilter([
        new EqualsFilter('id', 'Your example ID'),
        new EqualsFilter('name', 'Example name')
    ]));

    $products = $this->productRepository->search($criteria, $context);
}
```

To filter the association of productReviews where each review has a points value greater than or equal to 4, you can use `getAssociation` method and add a filter as shown in the following code snippet:

```php
public function readData(Context $context): void {
    // This will only return products that have at least one matching review with a points value of 4 or more.
    $criteria = new Criteria();
    $criteria->addFilter(new EqualsFilter('name', 'Example name'));
    $criteria->getAssociation('productReviews')->addFilter(new RangeFilter('points', [
        RangeFilter::GTE => 4
    ]));
    $products = $this->productRepository->search($criteria, $context)->first();
}
```

To add your own field to an existing entity like the `product` entity in a plugin, you need to create a custom extension class. Here's how you can do it:

1. Create a new directory for your extension under `<plugin root>/src/Extension/Content/Product/`. For example, if you're adding a string field named 'exampleExtension', the directory structure would look like this: `src/Extension/Content/Product/ExampleExtension`.

2. In this directory, create a class that extends from `Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityExtension`. For example:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Extension/Content/Product/ExampleExtension.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1)=
namespace Swag\BasicExample\Extension\Content\Product;
use Shopware\Core\Content\Product\ProductDefinition;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityExtension;

class ExampleExtension extends EntityExtension
{
    public function extendFields(FieldCollection $collection): void
    {
        $collection->add(
            (new OneToOneAssociationField('exampleExtension', 'id', 'product_id', ExampleExtensionDefinition::class, true))->addFlags(new CascadeDelete())
        );
    }
}
```

3. Define the `getEntityName` method to return the entity name of the product definition (`ProductDefinition::ENTITY_NAME`). This tells Shopware which entity you're extending.

4. Register your extension in the DI-container, as explained in greater detail in the [Add a custom class / service](../../services/add-custom-service.md) and [dependency injection](../../services/dependency-injection.md) guides.

This will add your new field to the product entity within your plugin's administration interface. For more details on extending entities with complex data, see the guide on [Adding Complex Data to Existing Entities](https://developer.shopware.com/docs/guides/plugins/plugins/framework/data-handling/add-complex-data-to-existing-entities).

If you need to add a field that requires saving it to the database, refer to the section in the same documentation for adding fields with a database.

To compute the average review rating of products using a DAL aggregation, you can use an `Avg` aggregation. Here's how you set it up in both PHP Criteria and API Criteria:

**PHP Criteria:"
```php
criteria = new Criteria();
criteria->setLimit(1);
criteria->addAggregation(new AvgAggregation('avg-review-rating', 'reviewRating'));
result = $repository->search($criteria, $context);
/** @var AvgResult $aggregation */
$aggregation = $result->getAggregations()->get('avg-review-rating');
$aggregation->getAvg();"

**API Criteria:"
```json
{
    "limit": 1,
    "includes": {
        "product": ["id", "name"]
    },
    "aggregations": [
 {   
            "name": "avg-review-rating",
            "type": "avg",
            "field": "reviewRating"
 }    ]
}
```

The response will include the average review rating for the products in your search result.
